That feeling's normal — DP has more of a structured template (states, transitions) once you've seen a few patterns. Greedy has no template; you have to *prove* to yourself the greedy choice is safe each time, which is why it feels harder.

Some things that build greedy intuition fast:

**1. Learn the recurring "shapes" of greedy proofs** — most greedy problems fall into a handful of buckets:
⁉️❓⁉️🙋‍♀️
- **Exchange argument** (what you just did in Advantage Shuffle) — "if I swap this choice for another, does it ever get worse?"
- **Sort + one pass** — job scheduling, interval problems (Meeting Rooms, Non-overlapping Intervals, Merge Intervals)
- **Two pointers on sorted arrays** — Boats to Save People, Assign Cookies, this problem
- **Heap-based greedy** — Task Scheduler, IPO, Reorganize String
- **Regret / undo greedy** — you pick greedily but keep a heap to "undo" a bad choice later
(e.g., Minimum Cost to Hire K Workers, Buy/Sell stock with fees variant)

Once you tag a new problem into one of these buckets, the approach becomes way less mysterious.

**2. Practice set** (good next problems, roughly increasing difficulty):
- Assign Cookies (LC 455) — warmup, two pointer greedy ✅
- Boats to Save People (LC 881) — two pointer, similar shape to what you just did ✅
- Jump Game II (LC 45) — greedy range extension ✅
- Gas Station (LC 134) — exchange argument, "if total works, one start point always works" ✅
- Task Scheduler (LC 621) — heap + greedy
- Non-overlapping Intervals (LC 435) — sort + greedy
- Candy (LC 135) — two-pass greedy, classic toughie

**3. When stuck, ask yourself explicitly:**
- "What's the worst thing that could go wrong if I make the locally best choice right now?"
- "Is there ever a case where being greedy *now* closes off a better option later?" — if you can't construct such a case, greedy's probably safe.

